.TH "ELEMENT.GETANIMATIONS" "3JS" "August 5, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Element.getAnimations \- Element: getAnimations() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[CR]getAnimations()\f[R] method of the \f[CR]Element\f[R]
interface returns an array of all \f[CR]Animation\f[R] objects affecting
this element, or that are scheduled to do so in the future.
It can optionally return \f[CR]Animation\f[R] objects either for
descendant elements and their pseudo\-elements or just for the specified
pseudo\-element.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This array includes CSS Animations, CSS Transitions, and
Web Animations.
.RE
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
getAnimations()
getAnimations(options)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]options\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
An options object containing the following properties:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]subtree\f[R]
A boolean value, which if \f[CR]true\f[R], causes animations that target
descendants of \f[I]Element\f[R] to be returned as well.
This includes animations that target any CSS pseudo\-elements attached
to \f[I]Element\f[R] or one of its descendants.
Defaults to \f[CR]false\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]pseudoElement\f[R]
A string that specifies a pseudo\-element to be the target element, such
as \f[CR]::after\f[R].
Note that specifying both \f[CR]pseudoElement\f[R] and
\f[CR]subtree\f[R] is equivalent to specifying just
\f[CR]pseudoElement\f[R].
.RE
.SS Return value
An \f[CR]Array\f[R] of \f[CR]Animation\f[R] objects, each representing
an animation currently targeting the \f[CR]Element\f[R].
.PP
If the \f[CR]{ subtree: true }\f[R] parameter is specified, the returned
value also includes animation objects targeting descendant elements,
including pseudo\-elements.
If \f[CR]options.pseudoElement\f[R] is specified, the return value
includes just the animation objects that match the selected
pseudo\-element.
.SS Exceptions
.TP
\f[B]SyntaxError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
An invalid pseudo\-element was passed in the
\f[CR]options.pseudoElement\f[R] parameter.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Wait for all animations on an element and its descendants
The following code snippet will wait for all animations on
\f[CR]elem\f[R] and its descendants to finish before removing the
element from the document.
.IP
.EX
Promise.all(
  elem.getAnimations({ subtree: \f[B]true\f[R] }).map((animation) \f[B]=>\f[R] animation.finished),
).then(() \f[B]=>\f[R] elem.remove());
.EE
.SS Get animations for a pseudo\-element target
This example displays a progress bar using a pseudo\-element.
It uses \f[CR]getAnimations()\f[R] to return the animations for the
pseudo\-element, starts them, and then removes the progress bar once the
animation is complete.
.PP
Note that the code uses a fallback approach to get the animations in
case the \f[CR]pseudoElement\f[R] option is not supported.
There is also hidden code to display a \(lqRestart\(rq button.
.SS HTML
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqprogress\-bar\(dq id=\(dqbar\(dq></\f[B]div\f[R]>
.EE
.SS CSS
The CSS styles the progress bar element to animate across the width of
its container in 3 seconds.
The animation is initially paused so that we can start it in JavaScript.
.IP
.EX
\&.progress\-bar {
  \f[B]width\f[R]: 100%;
  \f[B]height\f[R]: 20px;
  \f[B]background\f[R]: #eeeeee;
  \f[B]border\-radius\f[R]: 4px;
  \f[B]overflow\f[R]: hidden;
}

\&.progress\-bar\f[BI]::after\f[R] {
  \f[B]content\f[R]: \(dq\(dq;
  \f[B]display\f[R]: block;
  \f[B]height\f[R]: 100%;
  \f[B]width\f[R]: 0%;
  \f[B]background\f[R]: #4f46e5;
  \f[B]border\-radius\f[R]: 4px;
  \f[B]animation\f[R]: fill\-progress 3s ease\-in\-out forwards paused;
}

\f[B]\(atkeyframes\f[R] fill\-progress {
  from {
    \f[B]width\f[R]: 0%;
  }
  to {
    \f[B]width\f[R]: 100%;
  }
}
.EE
.SS JavaScript
First we define a function to get the animations associated with a
specified element and pseudo\-element.
It calls \f[CR]getAnimations()\f[R] with the \f[CR]pseudoElement\f[R]
option, and if that doesn\(cqt return any animations, falls back to
filtering the animations returned by \f[CR]subtree\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]function\f[R] getAnimationsForPseudo(element, pseudo) {
  \f[I]// Try the spec\-compliant way first (Firefox)\f[R]
  \f[B]try\f[R] {
    \f[B]const\f[R] anims = element.getAnimations({ pseudoElement: pseudo });
    \f[I]// If it returned something, the option is supported, so return the result\f[R]
    \f[B]if\f[R] (anims.length > 0) \f[B]return\f[R] anims;
  } \f[B]catch\f[R] (e) {
    \f[I]// invalid selector etc\f[R]
    \f[B]return\f[R] [];
  }

  \f[I]// Fallback for browsers that only support subtree\f[R]
  \f[B]return\f[R] element
    .getAnimations({ subtree: \f[B]true\f[R] })
    .filter((anim) \f[B]=>\f[R] anim.effect?.pseudoElement === pseudo);
}
.EE
.PP
We use this function to get all the animations associated with the
progress bar pseudo element.
The code iterates through the animations to start them, and then removes
the progress bar when all animations have finished.
Note that we run the code in \f[CR]requestAnimationFrame()\f[R] to
ensure that the animation is ready to run before our JavaScript is
executed.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] bar = document.getElementById(\(dqbar\(dq);

requestAnimationFrame(() \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  \f[B]const\f[R] anims = getAnimationsForPseudo(bar, \(dq::after\(dq);
  anims.forEach((a) \f[B]=>\f[R] a.play());
  Promise.all(anims.map((a) \f[B]=>\f[R] a.finished)).then(() \f[B]=>\f[R] bar.remove());
});
.EE
.SS Result
The bar should progress across the width of its container and then
disappear.
You can restart it by pressing the \(lqRestart\(rq button.
